    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q65-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $R_1=\\dfrac{R_2R}{R_2-R}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$R_1=\\dfrac{R_2R}{R_2-R}$.\n\nIsolate $\\frac{1}{R_1}$, combine the fractions, then take reciprocals.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\dfrac1{R_1}&=\\dfrac1R-\\dfrac1{R_2}\\\\\\dfrac1{R_1}&=\\dfrac{R_2-R}{RR_2}.\\end{align*}$$\nTaking reciprocals gives\n$$R_1=\\dfrac{R_2R}{R_2-R}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q65-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain why $R_2\\ne R$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $R_2=R$, the denominator $R_2-R$ in part (a) is zero, so $R_1$ is undefined.",
          "worked": "If $R_2=R$, the original relation gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\dfrac1{R_1}&=\\dfrac1R-\\dfrac1R\\\\\\dfrac1{R_1}&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThis is impossible for a finite nonzero resistance $R_1$. Equivalently, the denominator $R_2-R$ in part (a) would be zero. Therefore $R_2\\ne R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q65-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Let $d_1=R_1-R$ and $d_2=R_2-R$ be the difference between $R_1$ and $R_2$ respectively from $R$.\n\nShow that the product of the differences is always a constant value.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$d_1d_2=R^2$, constant for fixed equivalent resistance $R$.\n\nExpand the product, then use the identity from part (a).",
          "worked": "Part (a) gives $R_2R=R_1(R_2-R)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}d_1d_2&=(R_1-R)(R_2-R)\\\\d_1d_2&=R_1R_2-R_1R-R_2R+R^2\\\\d_1d_2&=R_1R_2-R_1R-R_1(R_2-R)+R^2\\\\d_1d_2&=R_1R_2-R_1R-R_1R_2+R_1R+R^2\\\\d_1d_2&=R^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the product is constant when the equivalent resistance $R$ is fixed. Its units are resistance squared.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      }
    ]
  },
